1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,614
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,634 --> 00:00:07,820
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:08,220 --> 00:00:14,382
This is Lecture 3, the needle-bar mechanism, a slider-crank.

4
00:00:15,902 --> 00:00:20,520
Let's start with a problem from the shop floor.

5
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:27,588
A factory runs its lockstitch machines at five thousand revolutions a minute,

6
00:00:27,608 --> 00:00:30,540
and production wants six thousand.

7
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:35,014
The maintenance team is worried about the needle bar.

8
00:00:35,034 --> 00:00:40,421
It slides up and down in its bushings more than eighty times a second.

9
00:00:40,821 --> 00:00:45,442
From experience they have set a limit for the bushings.

10
00:00:45,462 --> 00:00:52,361
The reciprocating inertia force at bottom dead centre must stay under 800 newtons.

11
00:00:52,761 --> 00:00:56,670
At six thousand, will it go over? And if it does,

12
00:00:56,690 --> 00:01:00,920
should we lighten the needle bar, or limit the speed?

13
00:01:01,320 --> 00:01:05,660
By the end of this lecture you'll be able to work it out.

14
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:11,971
The needle bar has a clear job. Carry the needle through the fabric,

15
00:01:11,991 --> 00:01:17,349
reach its lowest point at exactly the right moment, and do it at high speed.

16
00:01:17,749 --> 00:01:21,238
Here we're looking along the main shaft.

17
00:01:21,258 --> 00:01:26,150
The needle-bar crank sits on the front end of the shaft,

18
00:01:26,170 --> 00:01:30,449
with a counterweight cast opposite the crank pin.

19
00:01:30,849 --> 00:01:36,995
A link runs from the crank pin to a connecting stud clamped on the needle bar.

20
00:01:37,015 --> 00:01:43,082
A guide block on the stud runs in a slot, so the bar can only go up and down.

21
00:01:43,102 --> 00:01:44,189
It can't turn.

22
00:01:44,589 --> 00:01:50,353
There are two adjustments. Loosen the stud screw to set the needle-bar height,

23
00:01:50,373 --> 00:01:56,508
and loosen the needle clamp to set how deep the needle sits and which way it faces.

24
00:01:56,908 --> 00:02:01,891
Remember, these adjustments only move things. They don't change the motion.

25
00:02:01,911 --> 00:02:04,826
The motion is set by the crank and the link.

26
00:02:06,346 --> 00:02:09,806
Now draw it as a kinematic diagram.

27
00:02:10,206 --> 00:02:14,720
The main shaft is the crank OA, the link is AB,

28
00:02:14,740 --> 00:02:21,183
and the needle bar is the slider B. That's an in-line slider-crank.

29
00:02:21,583 --> 00:02:25,557
A car engine's piston is a slider-crank too,

30
00:02:25,577 --> 00:02:31,095
and you may have derived its formulas in a mechanisms course.

31
00:02:31,495 --> 00:02:36,215
But there's one difference. The piston sits above the crankshaft,

32
00:02:36,235 --> 00:02:39,715
while the needle bar hangs below the main shaft.

33
00:02:40,115 --> 00:02:46,807
At the top of its stroke the crank pin points up, away from the bar,

34
00:02:46,827 --> 00:02:50,855
so the distance from O to B is l minus r.

35
00:02:51,255 --> 00:02:56,056
So top and bottom dead centre are swapped compared with an engine.

36
00:02:56,076 --> 00:03:01,534
Copy the engine formulas straight over and you'll get the two accelerations

37
00:03:01,554 --> 00:03:02,995
the wrong way round.

38
00:03:03,395 --> 00:03:11,718
One more quantity runs through this whole lecture, the link ratio λ, r over l.

39
00:03:11,738 --> 00:03:19,099
In our example r is 15.5 millimetres and l is 55, so λ is about 0.28.

40
00:03:20,619 --> 00:03:24,712
Take top dead centre as zero, downward as positive,

41
00:03:24,732 --> 00:03:28,180
eliminate the link angle from the triangle,

42
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:31,406
and you get the needle-bar displacement.

43
00:03:31,806 --> 00:03:34,672
The first term comes from the crank,

44
00:03:34,692 --> 00:03:38,841
the second is the correction from the swinging link.

45
00:03:38,861 --> 00:03:44,934
Notice the second term has a minus sign, the opposite of the engine formula,

46
00:03:44,954 --> 00:03:48,541
and that's because the bar hangs upside down.

47
00:03:48,941 --> 00:03:51,657
With the shaft turning steadily,

48
00:03:51,677 --> 00:03:55,846
differentiate twice and you get the acceleration.

49
00:03:55,866 --> 00:04:00,975
At the two dead centres it's beautifully simple. At the top,

50
00:04:00,995 --> 00:04:07,387
r ω squared times one minus λ. At the bottom, r ω squared times one plus λ,

51
00:04:07,407 --> 00:04:10,721
with a minus sign because it points up.

52
00:04:11,121 --> 00:04:15,979
So the needle bar accelerates harder at the bottom than at the top.

53
00:04:15,999 --> 00:04:22,021
And the bottom is exactly when the needle is in the fabric and the loop is forming.

54
00:04:22,421 --> 00:04:27,738
Multiply by the reciprocating mass and you get the inertia force.

55
00:04:27,758 --> 00:04:29,791
And because ω is squared,

56
00:04:29,811 --> 00:04:34,881
twenty percent more speed means over forty percent more force.

57
00:04:36,401 --> 00:04:42,342
Let's see it move. On the left is the mechanism, on the right the displacement,

58
00:04:42,362 --> 00:04:47,021
velocity and acceleration curves, all from the exact formulas.

59
00:04:47,421 --> 00:04:49,216
Press Top dead centre.

60
00:04:49,236 --> 00:04:54,413
The crank pin points straight up and the bar is at its highest.

61
00:04:54,433 --> 00:04:58,951
The acceleration here is modest, and the curve is flat.

62
00:04:59,351 --> 00:05:01,120
Now Bottom dead centre.

63
00:05:01,140 --> 00:05:06,643
The crank points straight down and the bar turns round to come back up.

64
00:05:06,663 --> 00:05:11,855
The acceleration curve plunges to its deepest, sharpest point here,

65
00:05:11,875 --> 00:05:17,611
negative because it points up. This is where the inertia force is largest.

66
00:05:18,011 --> 00:05:19,708
Now Loop-catch moment.

67
00:05:19,728 --> 00:05:24,155
The needle has risen 2 millimetres from its lowest point,

68
00:05:24,175 --> 00:05:28,759
at a shaft angle of about 206 degrees. As we saw last time,

69
00:05:28,779 --> 00:05:32,271
this is when the hook point catches the loop.

70
00:05:32,671 --> 00:05:38,007
Now let it run. The bar lingers at the top and whips round at the bottom.

71
00:05:39,527 --> 00:05:46,003
Here are the three curves for our example. Look for three things.

72
00:05:46,403 --> 00:05:49,906
First, the displacement isn't symmetric.

73
00:05:49,926 --> 00:05:56,686
At 90 degrees the bar has moved only 13.3 millimetres, under half the stroke.

74
00:05:57,086 --> 00:06:02,789
Second, the peak velocity, 8.43 metres per second, comes at 104.7 degrees,

75
00:06:02,809 --> 00:06:08,202
not at 90. Both effects come from the swinging link, and the bigger λ,

76
00:06:08,222 --> 00:06:09,826
the bigger the shift.

77
00:06:10,226 --> 00:06:10,959
Third,

78
00:06:10,979 --> 00:06:20,493
the acceleration is about 3052 at the top and 5447 metres per second squared

79
00:06:20,513 --> 00:06:22,249
at the bottom.

80
00:06:22,269 --> 00:06:27,894
That's 555 times the acceleration of gravity.

81
00:06:28,294 --> 00:06:34,031
A needle-bar assembly of a hundred-odd grams carries hundreds of times its

82
00:06:34,051 --> 00:06:34,887
own weight,

83
00:06:34,907 --> 00:06:37,454
back and forth 83 times a second.

84
00:06:37,474 --> 00:06:41,188
That's what makes this mechanism hard to design.

85
00:06:42,708 --> 00:06:45,008
So how long should the link be?

86
00:06:45,408 --> 00:06:50,864
Keep the stroke fixed. The bigger λ, that is, the shorter the link,

87
00:06:50,884 --> 00:06:55,195
the faster the needle rises after bottom dead centre,

88
00:06:55,215 --> 00:06:57,892
and the earlier the hook catches.

89
00:06:58,292 --> 00:07:11,300
With λ at 0.2, 0.28 and 0.4, the catch comes at 206.9, 206.1 and 204.9 degrees.

90
00:07:11,320 --> 00:07:15,752
Only a degree or two apart.

91
00:07:16,152 --> 00:07:23,426
But as we found last lecture, the no-skip window is only about ten degrees,

92
00:07:23,446 --> 00:07:26,052
so a degree or two matters.

93
00:07:26,452 --> 00:07:28,542
Acceleration goes the same way.

94
00:07:28,562 --> 00:07:32,284
A shorter link means harder acceleration at the bottom.

95
00:07:32,304 --> 00:07:34,190
A longer link runs smoother,

96
00:07:34,210 --> 00:07:37,660
but the head gets taller and the parts get heavier.

97
00:07:38,060 --> 00:07:43,619
So the link ratio on an industrial machine is a compromise. And remember,

98
00:07:43,639 --> 00:07:46,905
change the crank radius or the link length,

99
00:07:46,925 --> 00:07:52,255
and you must re-set the hook mounting angle and the needle-bar height.

100
00:07:53,775 --> 00:07:56,250
Now back to our problem.

101
00:07:56,650 --> 00:08:01,024
With 120 grams reciprocating at five thousand,

102
00:08:01,044 --> 00:08:08,473
the force at bottom dead centre is about 654 newtons. That's inside the limit.

103
00:08:08,873 --> 00:08:13,951
At six thousand the speed is 1.2 times higher,

104
00:08:13,971 --> 00:08:21,709
so the force is 1.44 times higher, about 941 newtons. That's over 800.

105
00:08:22,109 --> 00:08:23,785
To keep six thousand,

106
00:08:23,805 --> 00:08:28,711
the reciprocating mass has to come down to 102 grams or less,

107
00:08:28,731 --> 00:08:30,569
about 18 grams lighter.

108
00:08:30,969 --> 00:08:34,067
Keep the parts as they are,

109
00:08:34,087 --> 00:08:40,189
and the top speed is about 5532 revolutions a minute.

110
00:08:40,589 --> 00:08:47,044
The usual engineering answer is to lighten the parts. A hollow needle bar,

111
00:08:47,064 --> 00:08:50,369
a small needle clamp, a pocketed link.

112
00:08:50,389 --> 00:08:54,569
Then re-check the balancing and the lubrication.

113
00:08:56,089 --> 00:09:03,067
But there's a counterweight on the crank. Why worry about inertia force at all?

114
00:09:03,467 --> 00:09:08,233
Split the force in two. The first-order part varies at shaft speed,

115
00:09:08,253 --> 00:09:12,804
and the counterweight's centrifugal force can cancel some of it.

116
00:09:12,824 --> 00:09:15,376
But that force turns with the crank,

117
00:09:15,396 --> 00:09:20,019
so cancelling the vertical part creates a new imbalance sideways.

118
00:09:20,039 --> 00:09:22,233
It can only balance part of it.

119
00:09:22,633 --> 00:09:28,375
The second-order part varies at twice shaft speed and is proportional to λ.

120
00:09:28,395 --> 00:09:31,602
The counterweight can do nothing about it.

121
00:09:31,622 --> 00:09:37,133
So the best way to cut vibration is still to cut the reciprocating mass.

122
00:09:37,533 --> 00:09:42,305
Robots work the same way. Run the same path in half the time,

123
00:09:42,325 --> 00:09:47,490
and joint accelerations and motor inertia torque go up four times.

124
00:09:47,510 --> 00:09:53,460
Shorten the cycle, and the first limit you hit is usually peak motor torque.

125
00:09:53,860 --> 00:09:55,501
And in everyday life,

126
00:09:55,521 --> 00:10:01,829
the hum you feel in a four-cylinder car at high revs is that second-order force.

127
00:10:01,849 --> 00:10:07,920
Some engines add a pair of balance shafts, turning at twice crankshaft speed,

128
00:10:07,940 --> 00:10:09,264
just to fight it.

129
00:10:10,784 --> 00:10:17,312
Now Virtual lab 3-1. On the WenQuest platform you can do it yourself.

130
00:10:17,332 --> 00:10:24,524
The numbers in the results panel are blurred here. Reading them is your job.

131
00:10:24,924 --> 00:10:28,349
Task one, change only the link length.

132
00:10:28,369 --> 00:10:35,784
Drag it to 90 millimetres and record the stroke and the dead-centre accelerations.

133
00:10:36,184 --> 00:10:43,326
Then try 35. The curves change shape, and the catching moment moves too.

134
00:10:43,346 --> 00:10:51,084
Task two is to set the link to 40 millimetres and read the new catching angle.

135
00:10:51,484 --> 00:10:57,048
Task three, take the speed from three thousand to six thousand,

136
00:10:57,068 --> 00:11:02,188
and see how many times bigger the peak inertia force gets.

137
00:11:02,208 --> 00:11:04,581
That checks the square law.

138
00:11:04,981 --> 00:11:10,166
Task four, at five thousand revolutions a minute,

139
00:11:10,186 --> 00:11:18,557
adjust the reciprocating mass until the peak force is no more than 400 newtons.

140
00:11:18,957 --> 00:11:22,996
Check every number against your own hand calculation.

141
00:11:23,016 --> 00:11:28,357
The grey dashed curves are the textbook example, there for comparison.

142
00:11:28,377 --> 00:11:32,417
I won't give the answers here. They're yours to find.

143
00:11:32,817 --> 00:11:36,962
The lab comes with a guide and a report template.

144
00:11:36,982 --> 00:11:42,997
Download them on the platform and hand in your report when you're done.

145
00:11:44,517 --> 00:11:50,426
Numbers worked out in design have to be watched with data in the factory.

146
00:11:50,826 --> 00:11:55,143
This is the quality loop in the WenQuest digital factory.

147
00:11:55,163 --> 00:12:00,317
The adjustment station measures the hook clearance on every machine,

148
00:12:00,337 --> 00:12:04,121
the readings go over the bus into a control chart,

149
00:12:04,141 --> 00:12:08,990
and when the data drifts the system raises an alarm and drafts a

150
00:12:09,010 --> 00:12:13,326
nonconformance report for a quality inspector to confirm.

151
00:12:13,726 --> 00:12:19,041
You can pick the detection rules yourself, change the drift rate,

152
00:12:19,061 --> 00:12:22,899
and see how many hours earlier the alarm comes.

153
00:12:23,299 --> 00:12:26,433
The second page is the serial-number and batch trace.

154
00:12:26,453 --> 00:12:28,753
From the machine that raised the alarm,

155
00:12:28,773 --> 00:12:33,394
back to the hook batch it was built with and the grinder that made that batch.

156
00:12:33,414 --> 00:12:37,143
Then the other way, to every machine built with the same batch,

157
00:12:37,163 --> 00:12:39,345
and whether any have already shipped.

158
00:12:39,745 --> 00:12:40,830
Today's rule,

159
00:12:40,850 --> 00:12:46,439
re-set the hook and the needle-bar height whenever r or l changes,

160
00:12:46,459 --> 00:12:50,858
is checked and traced like this, machine by machine.

161
00:12:50,878 --> 00:12:54,428
We'll walk through it fully in Lecture 27.

162
00:12:55,948 --> 00:12:58,128
Your assignment has four questions.

163
00:12:58,528 --> 00:13:04,067
One, calculation. For a new r, l and speed, find the stroke,

164
00:13:04,087 --> 00:13:08,977
the dead-centre accelerations and the catching angle,

165
00:13:08,997 --> 00:13:11,386
and check them in the lab.

166
00:13:11,786 --> 00:13:15,042
Two, design. Today's shop-floor problem.

167
00:13:15,062 --> 00:13:19,548
Give the numbers for lightening and for limiting speed,

168
00:13:19,568 --> 00:13:22,006
and say which you'd recommend.

169
00:13:22,406 --> 00:13:25,180
Three, analysis. Shorten the link.

170
00:13:25,200 --> 00:13:30,277
How do the catching moment and the bottom acceleration change,

171
00:13:30,297 --> 00:13:33,236
and how should the hook be adjusted?

172
00:13:33,636 --> 00:13:35,151
Four, short answer.

173
00:13:35,171 --> 00:13:41,696
Give two reasons why a counterweight can't fully balance the reciprocating force.

174
00:13:43,216 --> 00:13:49,417
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example, at its highest point,

175
00:13:49,437 --> 00:13:52,436
what is the distance from O to B?

176
00:13:52,836 --> 00:13:56,785
Shorten the link, and does the catch come earlier or later?

177
00:13:57,185 --> 00:14:03,005
You get three attempts, and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

178
00:14:04,525 --> 00:14:06,745
Three things to take away.

179
00:14:07,145 --> 00:14:12,068
One. The needle bar is an upside-down slider-crank,

180
00:14:12,088 --> 00:14:16,624
with its dead centres swapped from an engine's.

181
00:14:17,024 --> 00:14:21,791
Two. The acceleration is largest at bottom dead centre,

182
00:14:21,811 --> 00:14:26,056
and inertia force grows with the square of speed.

183
00:14:26,076 --> 00:14:31,103
Twenty percent faster means over forty percent more force.

184
00:14:31,503 --> 00:14:37,268
Three. The counterweight can't touch the second-order force.

185
00:14:37,288 --> 00:14:41,317
Cutting the reciprocating mass works best.

186
